    "body": "EICMA 2026 • 2026 EICMA's Speed Legends Exhibit Was Not To Be Missed – See the Bikes That Paved MotoGP History\nJapanese Air Taxis to Start Operating in Australia’s Gold Coast by 2028\n7 photos\nPhoto: SkyDrive\nJapan’s best-known eVTOL (electric vertical takeoff and landing) maker has reached the second phase of a major Australian collaboration aimed at introducing regular air taxi service across the famous Gold Coast, which could happen as early as 2028.\nSkyDrive, maker of the compact SD-05 passenger-carrying electric air taxi, derived from the even smaller SD-03, once considered the most compact eVTOL in the world, is taking big steps toward commercial operations in one of the most important Aussie cities. The second-largest city in Queensland, and one of the most popular coastal destinations for tourists from all over the world, the Gold Coast is a perfect launch pad for urban air taxi operations.\nThis is a large-scale project, given that SkyDrive has now established official partnerships with both a private-sector player and the local government. In other words, it’s not just about delivering a few aircraft, but working with authorities and the local industry to create an entire AAM (Advanced Air Mobility) ecosystem.\nEarlier this summer, SkyDrive first signed a LOI (Letter of Intent) with aviation operator Gold Coast Helitours. That was followed by a MoU (memorandum of understanding) signed with the City of Gold Coast, this month.\nIf things go according to plan, the Japanese SD-05 could herald Australia’s first official air taxi operations, and the big deadline that everyone is looking toward is the 2032 Summer Olympics, already known as Brisbane 2032. Although Brisbane is the official host, many of the sporting events that year will extend to the Gold Coast and other locations, and that’s an excellent opportunity to already have an air taxi system in place.\nThat is why SkyDrive and its partners are hoping to launch commercial operations here as soon as 2028, which would give them enough time to have everything working smoothly by 2032. This includes fully operational vertiports at multiple locations, maintenance bases, and confirmed routes for air taxi transportation and tourism, connecting the City of Gold Coast with other important hubs in the region.\nThings really took off for the Japanese eVTOL maker after the successful demonstration flights at Expo 2025 Osaka, in Japan. It is currently the only AAM company in Japan to have reached this far in terms of type certification with the Japan Civil Aviation Bureau (JCAB), and its market success counts confirmed orders for more than 450 aircraft.\nA lot of this success has to do with Suzuki being a strategic partner and investor. Recently, SkyDrive has confirmed that it’s gearing up to kick off mass production at a Suzuki facility in Japan, and the Group has also been instrumental in introducing the SD-05 eVTOL on major international markets, like India and the US.\nEarlier this month, Japan’s Finance Minister attended a SkyDrive flight demonstration and was impressed with how quiet the electric aircraft was, and how quickly it was able to change direction. The SD-05 is primarily built for short-distance urban flights of around 15 km (9 miles) at a top speed of 100 kph (62 mph).\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\njapanese evtol SkyDrive SD-05 eVTOL evtol air taxi\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Otilia Drăgan\nOtilia believes that if it’s eco, green, or groundbreaking, people should know about it (especially if it's got wheels or wings). Working in online media for over five years, she's gained a deeper perspective on how people everywhere can inspire each other. Full profile\nUP NEXT New Owner Repaints All-Original 1969 Camaro Z/28 Time Capsule, Internet Is Enraged, He Sells It",
